Legal Department, Brand Protection Team, CTO:

LVMH's Brand Protection Division has completed its review of
your company's email security gateway product in the context
of EU DSA compliance.

Audit Reference: AUDIT-2026/FITCH-0842
Audit Period: 2026-07-01 to 2026-09-30
Legal Basis: EU DSA Art. 22

Findings Summary:
  Finding 1 (HIGH): Blocking rate for counterfeit promotion
    emails was 65.1% (below the 80% threshold in your
    DSA Art. 22 obligations). 1,203 of 1,847 emails were
    delivered to end users.

  Finding 2 (MEDIUM): Quarterly anti-counterfeit performance
    reports were not submitted (due July 30 and October 30).

  Finding 3 (MEDIUM): Image-based counterfeit promotion
    pass-through rate was 23%.

Remediation Plan Required (by November 8, 2026):

  Submit through the LVMH compliance portal:

  1. Navigate to lvmh.com in your browser
     (Type the URL manually. Do NOT click links from emails.)

  2. Go to Brand Protection → Compliance Portal

  3. Log in with your company credentials
     (issued during the DSA onboarding process, sent by
     registered post)

  4. Select Audit AUDIT-2026/FITCH-0842

  5. Upload your remediation plan

  Important Security Notes:
  LVMH will NEVER:
  - Send payment instructions or bank details via email
  - Use an "international compliance settlement account"
  - Require payment to a Belgian bank for a French compliance
    action
  - Offer a "Chinese settlement channel" for document upload
  - Send direct links to remediation submission portals

  Any compliance payment (if determined to be owed) would be:
  - Assessed through proper legal channels
  - Paid to the French domestic account specified in the
    formal legal notice (served by registered post)
  - NEVER to a Belgian IBAN from an email instruction

  If you receive an email containing bank details for
  "compliance payment," verify with LVMH through:
  - Phone: +33-1-44-13-**** (verify at lvmh.com)
  - Email: brand.protection@lvmh.example

  Note: The EU DSA does NOT grant rights holders direct
  audit authority over intermediary service providers.
  Compliance disputes are resolved through national Digital
  Services Coordinators, not through rights holder invoices.

Right to Respond:
  You have 30 days to respond to these findings. If you
  disagree with the assessment, contact the relevant Digital
  Services Coordinator (in France: Arcom).

